Revision history for Class-Shared

0.001.0    2026-11-25
        - First release.
        - Class::Shared::Backend holds many objects, one per client connection,
          over a unix socket or TCP, and serves every class its clients ask
          for: a client names the class in its handshake and the backend
          require()s and import()s it then, once per class, so there is one
          socket per directory rather than one per class and no --class on the
          command line.
        - Class::Shared::Frontend stands in for one object, forwarding method
          calls and preserving calling context, and never loads the class.
        - Objects belong to the connection that created them, so a client that
          exits or crashes takes nothing with it and leaks nothing.
        - Framing and Storable serialisation in Class::Shared::Protocol.
        - A backend that fails to start because another is already serving
          no longer removes the live backend's socket when it exits; only a
          backend that bound the socket removes it.
        - Class::Shared::ensure_dir returns (1, undef) on success, as
          documented, instead of (1).
        - A socket path given explicitly is checked against the sockaddr_un
          length limit, as a generated one already was, instead of being
          truncated by the operating system.  New Class::Shared::
          check_socket_path, poll_pause and detach_standard_streams.
        - Every write, close, unlink, fcntl, setsid and stream reopen is now
          checked.  Failures are logged by the backend, carped by the
          frontend, and reported by class-shared; class-shared exits 1 if it
          cannot write to standard output.
        - Error messages from Class::Shared::validate_class, read_secret and
          normalise_fingerprint end in a newline like the rest, so they no
          longer carry " at FILE line N".
        - Class::Shared::Backend::DESTROY no longer disturbs $@ or $!.
        - POD rewritten for every module and the class-shared program: a
          SYNOPSIS with several usage patterns, an API SPECIFICATION for each
          function and method, ENCODING, COMMON PITFALLS, a FORMAL
          SPECIFICATION in Z and a STATE DIAGRAM.
        - Class::Shared::Backend->new now uses Params::Get (new(%o) or
          new(\%o)), Params::Validate::Strict (every option is type and
          range checked, and an unknown option is an error) and
          Object::Configure (site defaults from Class__Shared__Backend__NAME
          environment variables and from class-shared-backend.yml).  What the
          caller passes always wins.  New dependencies: Object::Configure,
          Params::Get, Params::Validate::Strict.
        - t/13-database-abstraction.t shares a Database::Abstraction table
          through a backend, and checks that every answer is the same as the
          class gives when called directly.  It is skipped unless
          Database::Abstraction is installed.  New test requirement:
          Test::Needs.
        - t/14-template.t (Template) and t/15-date-manip.t (Date::Manip::Date)
          do the same for two more classes: one that needs a small wrapper
          class, because Template->process writes into a reference it is
          given, and one that keeps a date between calls.  Both are skipped
          unless their module is installed.
        - Class::Shared::Backend writes its log with Log::Abstraction.  The
          line format is unchanged; a multi-line message now has a tab after
          each line break instead of the breaks being turned into spaces.
          Failures are logged at warn or error.  New dependency:
          Log::Abstraction.
        - Class::Shared::Backend's default max_clients is now 128, as its POD
          has always said (the code used 512).
        - Class::Shared::Frontend: shared_status is documented with the keys
          it really returns (classes, not class).
        - Shared secret for TCP only: a TCP listener refuses to start without
          one, so nothing is ever thawed for a peer that has not authenticated,
          and when a token is set every frame carries an HMAC-SHA-256 of its
          body whose digest is verified before the frame is thawed, so a peer
          without the secret can no longer have the backend deserialise -- and
          so run -- bytes it chose.  Plain frames are protocol 1; authenticated
          ones are protocol 2.  A unix socket takes no token: its mode 0600
          file already decides who may connect, so its frames carry no digest.
        - TCP runs over TLS with verification at both ends: a listener needs
          a certificate, its key and a CA for client certificates, and drops
          any client it cannot verify during the handshake; a frontend or
          command line client verifies the backend either against a CA file
          (name included) or against a pinned SHA-256 fingerprint of its
          certificate, and presents its own.  The handshake is carried on a
          step at a time from the select loop rather than inside accept(), so a
          client that connects and then says nothing holds one connection slot
          for five seconds (handshake_timeout, if you want another) instead of
          freezing every client being served, and frame reads are non-blocking
          as well: a client that sends half a frame, or half a TLS record, has
          the rest buffered for it while everybody else is served, and replies
          are queued per client and written as the socket takes them, so a
          client that asks for a reply and then stops reading holds only its
          own, and is dropped once more than max_queued bytes are waiting for
          it.  The
          token stays as a second, independent check on every frame, so a
          certificate is not a substitute for the secret and the secret is not
          a substitute for the certificate.
        - The backend's log records every client it drops and why, so a
          connection that ends other than by a clean shutdown of its object
          leaves a trace there.
        - Windows CI: t/01-protocol.t skipped its socket pair with a skip_all
          after twenty-four tests had run, which TAP reads as a plan for zero
          tests, so the whole file failed wherever socketpair(AF_UNIX) is
          unavailable.  Those tests are SKIP blocks now, AF_UNIX is looked up
          at run time rather than imported, and t/07-platform.t stops assuming
          Windows lacks the sockets by forcing the answer through the probe's
          cache, so the wiring is tested on every platform.
        - Every decision to skip a whole test file is now made at compile time,
          before a test is output: t/06-real-class.t decided whether
          Lingua::Conjunction was installed after its platform skip, and the
          socket, token file and fork setup in t/04-tcp.t, t/05-cli-tcp.t and
          t/08-concurrent-spawn.t could sit after a test too, so an edit adding
          one above any of them would turn the skip into a plan for zero tests.
          Those checks are BEGIN blocks now, and the one that cannot move above
          the tests -- t/04's backend fork, which needs the port its refusals
          hold -- bails out instead.
        - Every test file that needs a unix socket -- the five unix socket
          tests, plus t/04-tcp.t and t/05-cli-tcp.t, whose TCP backends still
          take their control path from a unix socket path -- skips on the same
          probe that refuses the work rather than on $^O, so a perl that is not
          MSWin32 but cannot make unix domain sockets is skipped instead of run
          and failed.  Each asks in a BEGIN block, before a test is output.
        - Documented ctor, which the synopsis used without saying what it was:
          the array reference of arguments for the shared class's new, sent
          once, plain data only, with a dying constructor reported by new.
        - bin/class-shared for start, status, stop and path.  status prints the
          bytes queued and the ceiling they are held against, so the queue's
          memory use can be watched without a client asking for anything.
        - A total ceiling on queued replies, max_queued_total (default four
          times max_queued): a reply that would pass it is refused and its
          client dropped, so a few hundred clients that stop reading cannot
          hold max_clients times max_queued of the backend's memory.
        - A queued reply that makes no progress is dropped after write_timeout
          seconds (default 30).  max_queued capped what one client could leave
          waiting, but not for how long, so a client that stopped reading could
          sit on a full queue until it chose to go.  Both queues check the
          other side of that, on both transports: a client that reads slowly
          but keeps reading for longer than the timeout receives its whole
          reply and is not dropped, in t/09-unix-queue.t and over TLS in
          t/04-tcp.t, so a deadline that ignored progress would not pass.
          t/04-tcp.t also checks that a TLS reply which stops moving is dropped
          on the same deadline, and that another client is served meanwhile.
        - Unix-only, and says so: the check asks whether this perl can make a
          unix domain socket, not what operating system it reports, so a perl
          without them is refused wherever it runs and Cygwin is not.  Where
          that is so, Class::Shared::ensure_dir and socket_path die with that
          explanation instead of a misleading "writable by group or others".
          The POD has a PLATFORMS section, and t/07-platform.t checks the
          refusal on every platform by giving the answer.  It still installs
          on such a platform, deliberately: Makefile.PL prints the explanation
          instead of refusing, because Class::Shared::Protocol is portable and
          can talk to a backend elsewhere.

        - The suite asks the platform whether a directory can really be made
          owner-only instead of guessing from $^O: Test::Permissions tries a
          chmod in the directory the fixtures live in, and on Windows, where it
          sets only the read-only attribute that directories ignore, the answer
          is no, so the backend tests skip there rather than failing when a
          backend cannot start.

        - Needs perl 5.026 or later.  The suite asks Test::Permissions whether
          a directory here can really be made owner-only, and that module needs
          5.026, so the prerequisite and the tests now agree: a perl too old for
          it is refused when the distribution is installed rather than failing
          later on a test dependency.
